#:lesson{:id :lesson/grade6.patterns.visualizing,
         :title "Visualising Number Sequences",
         :grade-level 6,
         :domain :patterns,
         :concepts
         #{:concept/geometric-numbers :concept/number-sequence},
         :skills
         #{:skill/patterns.relate-sequences
           :skill/patterns.visualize-numbers},
         :blocks
         [#:block{:title "Visualising Number Sequences",
                  :type :explain,
                  :body
                  "Many number sequences can be visualised using pictures. Visualising mathematical objects through pictures or diagrams can be a very fruitful way to understand mathematical patterns and concepts."}
          #:block{:title "Square Numbers",
                  :type :explain,
                  :body
                  "The sequence 1, 4, 9, 16, 25... is called \"Square numbers\". \nThis is because 4 dots can form a 2x2 square, 9 dots can form a 3x3 square, and 16 dots can form a 4x4 square!"}
          #:block{:title "Triangular Numbers",
                  :type :explain,
                  :body
                  "The sequence 1, 3, 6, 10, 15... is called \"Triangular numbers\".\nIf you stack rows of dots starting with 1 dot on top, then 2 dots, then 3 dots... you form a triangle!\nRow 1 = 1\nRow 2 = 1 + 2 = 3\nRow 3 = 1 + 2 + 3 = 6"}
          #:block{:title "Dual Representation",
                  :type :example,
                  :prompt
                  "Can a number be both a square and a triangle?",
                  :expression "Look at the number 36.",
                  :answer
                  "Yes! 36 is 6x6 (a square) and it's also the 8th triangular number (1+2+3+4+5+6+7+8 = 36)."}
          #:block{:title "Relations among Number Sequences",
                  :type :explain,
                  :body
                  "Sometimes, number sequences can be related to each other in surprising ways.\nWhat happens when we start adding up odd numbers?\n1 = 1\n1 + 3 = 4\n1 + 3 + 5 = 9\n1 + 3 + 5 + 7 = 16\n\nAdding up odd numbers gives you the Square Numbers! This beautiful pattern happens forever, and by drawing the dots in an L-shape around a square, you can visually prove it."}
          #:block{:title "Practice Time",
                  :type :practice-ref,
                  :exercise-set-id
                  :exercise-set/grade6.patterns.visualizing}]}
